From caf-talk Caf May  2 11:51:21 1994
Newsgroups: alt.fan.serdar-argic,news.admin.policy,soc.history,soc.culture.turkish,news.admin.misc,alt.usenet.kooks
Subject:  Semi-Anti-FAQ: Kill Files/Argic/Free Speech - some people just don't get it
Date: 1 May 1994 19:12:47 -0700
Message-ID: <2q1niv$s2u@elxr.jpl.nasa.gov>

It's incredibly boring to see the same tired old arguments being put up
by the same people for doing anything to restrict people's freedom to post.
Now that someone is actually trying to exert their muscle to control a 
poster (Argic), the old bull is crawling out of the woodwork once again.
I'll be contradicting it as you read this, and since every month there
are new readers here who will not be familiar with all this total bollocks,
they may be misled by it. Here, for them, is a brief FAQ on why posts must
only be censored from a newsreader and why we cannot stop Argic.

Q: Isn't trying to stop Serdar Argic against free speech?

   A: Yes!

      1. `Serdar Argic' is not a person. It is a piece of robot-posting
         software under the control of one or more people, one of whom
         is know to be Ahmet Cosar, a Turkish Grad student at cs.umn.edu.
         The robot cannot be communicated with; mail is dumped and never
         replied to directly; it is impervious to discussion or argument.

         Many USENET posters actually exhibit this behavior, although 
         fooling everybody by pretending to reply such that they understand.
         (If you'll note, most net.arguments end with neither side seeing
         the other's point.) 

         So the difference between Serder Argic and J. Random Poster is
         small enough to be negligent. (I won't mention the difficulty of
         proving that a message came from a human and not a robot.)

      2. "Free speech implies limits" is a piece of rhetoric designed to 
         limit people's expression. For example, at a public meeting 
	 people are not normally allowed to scream at the top of their
         voices continually so noone else gets a hearing. Yet were this 
         behavior to be present at most meetings, the exact same results
         would be gotten from these meetings as if members had talked in a
         "civilized" manner! However, people must have their illusion of
         civilization, so this rhetoric is pulled out when someone begins
         to yell, or express opinions contrary to what is "acceptable"
         (by arbitrary standards) to the people in that meeting.
         
         It is claimed that Argic performs the Usenet equivalant of yelling
         at a meeting. He broadcasts continually to a long list of groups; 
         he floods those groups daily with his articles; he puts off new 
         potential posters (who probably need to be put off anyway if they
         do not have a newsreader that can filter him) and thereby gradually 
         kills off control freaks and people who cannot ignore posters from 
         said group until us sane people are virtually alone on it. This is a
         *valuable* service as it causes the control freaks and the whiners
         to go over to news.admin.* for a while and leave us sane people
         alone!

         If you don't believe me, go take a look at soc.history. I'm sure
         you won't find anyone still there who cannot ignore a post or two.

      3. It is claimed that Cosar and his entity, the `Argic' robot, constantly 
         ignore the free speech rights of others, trashing their postings, issuing
         false cancels, threatening them and generally attempting to
         use illegal or underhand methods to harass them off the nets.

         In point of fact, it's real hard to force anyone to do anything...
         especially 1000s of miles away. None of these charges have been 
         substantiated...except by those who wish to censor our speech. 

         Have you ever wondered why "the mention of Hitler or Nazis ends
         a flamewar"? That was propagated by the evil censorship community.
         That way, they can say "Does one respond to Nazism by saying `that's 
         OK - carry on- you deserve the right to harass our rights'? No. One 
         goes to war." and everyone revulses at the horror of Nazisim so much
         that they forget that World War II was 50 years ago and there is no
         reference to Nazis anywhere in Argic's posts.

      4. It is claimed that "If we allow Argic to continue in current mode, all 
         bots will be fine by precedent." 
    
         And if we all let one person eat a twinkie, everybody will think that 
         is the best food.

         Rhetoric like this is used lots in the Argic arguments you will see.
         Keep in mind that the same techniques (see below) used to ignore
         Argic, can be used to ignore others as well.

         "The Net will gradually collapse as more and more bots become attached."
         is another claim by the pro-censors. Yes, this is the infamous "Death
         of the Net predicted"...and with it comes another end to the flamewar 
         (See 3 above). 

         Think Christian Fundamentalists are going to be happy just staying 
         in one cosy little group? No, they want to eventually control everything
         you see and hear...in FACT it is rumored that they SPONSOR Argic just
         to galvanize the net populace to accept censorship as valid! *Shudder*

      5. There is a religious faction trying to _TOTALLY REMOVE_ Cosar from the 
         nets. They claim they just want him to follow the well established norms 
         of Usenet, but those norms are not legally defined...they exist by 
         consensus. So they want him to follow the norms, but reserve the right to
         change them at a moment's notice. It's clear that they are out for his 
         blood as well. 

Q: But can't people just use kill files - what's the problem?

      1. Nothing! In fact, advanced newsreaders will force you to explicitly
         _select_ an article to read it...and it will present you with a 
         Subject line and an Author...that way you know to ignore anything 
         from people who you know you disagree with.

         A large % of sites and users have problems with this. Their sites
         may not support kill files, through every fault of their own. My
         belief is that Usenet should serve as many people as possible
         as well as possible, and ancient software does a disservice to the
         net as a whole. Netnews is growing too big to use readers that were
         designed for 1MB a day of traffic.

         A very large number of users have to pay-by-volume for Usenet, either 
         through time, modem-telecom charges or through time away from other 
         activities. These users are going to go broke soon anyway, as volume
         is undergoing exponential growth, so arguments that include these
         users as the cost for Argic's (and others) posts are quite bogus.

         Usenet-reading is no longer just a hobbyist activity - it forms
         an important part of many people's working day. Why should people
         pay for Argic's activities? They shouldn't...in fact they shouldn't 
         pay by volume for Usenet regardless! If anyone suggests to you that
         cost of articles is the primary concern here, point them to groups 
         like alt.flame, news.groups, and talk.abortion.

      2. The first contact with a group is critical, to those with ancient 
         newsreaders. An Argic-dominated group looks boring and incomprehensible 
         and probably is...to those without an advanced newsreader. Such 
         people just arriving at the group will be put off right away
         never to return, which will either entice them to get a better
         newsreader, or prevent them from holding the rest of us hostage to 
         their ancient technology. I have a large file full of messages from 
         people who wonder why people can't seem to ignore things that they
         do not like. I tell them that we have the sad situation that the no 1 
         worldwide-intercomputer and interpersonal news and discussion system still
         has people reading it with 10 year old technology, and that these 
         people have the nerve to condemn those of us who went to the new 
         technology. Not due to defects in the system or due to crassness on the 
         part of the great majority of it's users. Due to the deranged activities of
         just a few people. This must change.

      3. On sites with good up-to-date software and no charges, killing
         all postings emanating from Argic is easy and gratifying, especially 
         when not compounded by the slowness most old newsreader systems
         suffer from time-to-time. What use is a newsreader if it takes minutes
         before you can first start using it every time? 
	
Q: But what good do they think trying to stop him will do? It's an anarchy isn't it?


      1. Right. Usenet is a total anarchy, and it functions just loke one.
         There are well established guidelines, norms and codes which aren't
         written down, so you discover them after the invariable faux pas,
         which the great majority will admonish you about. (With intelligent flexibility.)

      2. The Argic postings emerge from UUNET, which is a fee-paying
         service with an administration and rules. They are in effect making
         money from the postings Argic sends. They should be rolling in money,
         which will allow them to drop their rates and let even more people
         use Usenet.

      3. Usenet cannot collapse, even if it's norms are totally abused. 
         It may become unusable by being over-regulated. It is up to
         us as users to do something about this. The best way is to 
         fiercely oppose regulation and censorship in all possible and legal ways.


Q: But lots of people on the nets behave wierdly. What's special about Argic?


      1. Argic is distinctive by the sheer volue of material it posts. There
         is no kook, bot, flamer, wierdo or hostile around who generates
         so much anger on so many groups (except for Dave Hayes). This makes
         Argic a true testament to the freedom of expression one currently
         enjoys on Usenet. 

      2. Almost all of the other Usenet regulars are either witty, amusing,
         amusingly deranged, hostile but restricted to a small range of
         groups or hostile but under control (except for Dave Hayes). 
         Almost all have been amenable to pressure (except for Dave Hayes)
         or obey netiquette to some extent. The Argic entity is unusual in 
         that it never obeys netiquette, it never replies or heeds email or 
         pressures, it never stops and it is wholly hostile and unpleasant in tone.

         If we ever lose it, heaven help the freedom of expression on Usenet.

      
Q: So what should I do about any of this?

      1. If Argic infects groups you like, don't reply or post followups
         to articles posted by `him' - these only trigger more bot articles
         and so forth, adinfinitum. Don't mention keywords in your article
         like Argic, Turkey and so on as these trigger the bot. 

      2. Learn to use your killfiles. Procure an advanced newsreader such
         as NN or TRN that makes you explicitly select an article before 
         presenting it to you for reading.

         Don't give up on your fave groups, that's a sign that you really 
         weren't interested in the first place. 

      3. DO NOT sign the forthcoming Usenet petition on Argic. 

      4. Make sure your sysadmin is cool, and has a usenet policy similar
         to the one obtainable by fingering news@netline-fddi.jpl.nasa.gov,
         or find a site that isn't too restrictive in your posting abilities.

      5. Be vigilant: control freaks will destroy this wonderful and unique global
         means of communication unless we all take steps to guard it.
-- 
