Freedom of speech is a privilege and a responsibility.  In a society that
reveres free speech, I find it appalling that accounts would be yanked
because
one person or one group of persons doesn't like what someone has to say.
If you don't like what someone has to say, then delete it.  It's simple,
it's on your keyboard and even a computer bonehead like me can do it.

I am not fond of everything I read here.  That doesn't mean that when I
read 
Gregg, Don or Marek blowtorching each other into the next dimension that 
I am going to get their accounts yanked to shut them up.  It means that I
will open their missives, discover that I don't like it and then chuck it.
Just like the ever constant flow of junk mail into my mail box --  I take
it 
to the dump.  I don't always find that what they have to say is junk and
sometimes I even enjoy what I read;  what this means is that I have the
responsibility of screening my own reading to my own tastes.  My privilege.

If I post here, it means that I am preparing to take the bad with the good.
There are always going to be people that would rather choke than read my
writing.  There are always going to be people I would rather choke than
read.  However, being given the opportunity to post in this forum means
that I have to be tolerant of people who hate and love what I write, and
whose writing I love to hate, etc., etc., etc.

If someone doesn't like my poetry, I would hope that they would take the
time to tell me why it sucked.  That doesn't always happen, but I will roll
with it.  Freedom of speech means that I have the freedom to filter out
what is good and bad for me.  My standards may or may not reflect someone
else's, which means that if you don't like what I write, you don't have to
read it and vice versa.  Unless someone is threatening me with bodily harm,
or doing something illegal with my account, I can see no reason why anyone
who posts here should be under threat of harrassment to have their account 
yanked.  If we are going to start running a police state, we should, in 
addition, jail people for not agreeing with us.  Which means, of course,
that we should each have our own little state -- because we are all 
individuals with a different opinion on every topic. 

I've only been here for a couple months, and I have seen evidence that 3
accounts were yanked and 3 people have left (welcome back, Zita) for
various reasons.  People leaving for harrassment is unacceptable, as is 
pestering postmasters into yanking accounts.  If you don't like what you
read, you have the option of dumping into a virtually real garbage bin.
If someone posted pro-Nazi poems, I would be irate about them, but I would
defend to the death, that person's right to post.  I would also defend to
the death my right to respond to the post and/or ignore it.

I think it is necessary to reexamine what freedom of speech means, and 
be responsible readers.  If someone lights a flame, you can add fuel to
the fire, i.e., give your energy to that, or ignore it, which means that 
it will burn itself out.  

Take what you can use and disregard the rest,


Wendy
  

