I must admit to some ambiguities in making the names of the accused public
prior to a conviction.

The reason for the requirement in the U.S. wasn't to aid shows like
"Current Affair" or to help publishers sell newspapers.  It was to ensure
that the accused received a public trial and that the accused was not held
incommunicado.  If arrests are not a matter of public record, you could be
arrested and detained and no one might be able to find out.  Without a
public trial, all sorts of improprieties could takeplace and no one would
be the wiser.

Publication of the accused's name and selected "facts" of the case pose a
risk to the fairness of a trial.  Courts sometimes take that into account.
 More important to me is the damage to the accused after the acquittal. 
Their reputation is forever marred.  But the whole process is painful for
the accused.  They have lost earnings, lawyers to pay, etc.  The accused
can lose everything they own just to get an acquittal.  That's also one of
the reasons they sometime plead guilty to something they did not do.  It's
often cheaper and less painful in the long-run.

Mitch

