Message-ID: <m0pyMtB-000Bc8C@mercury.mcs.com>

May 3, 1994, Chicago, Illinois


[This letter was made available to several individuals and rec.arts.poems]

Hello Postmaster at Math.Clemson.Edu:

It is public knowledge (cf. rec.arts.poems, recently) that Barbara Taylor
has written you to accomplish the revocation of Prof. Coffin's Internet
access, as ludicrous as that may sound.  This action had the unexpected
and highly harmful effect of having Prof. Coffin withdraw altogether from
rec.arts.poems, where she is easily one of the very most gifted poets,
critics and all-around good folks, with plenty of meritorious contributions.

As a co-author with Prof. Coffin, I am personally injured by this loss.

I ask that you make public the content of the letter from Barbara Taylor
so that all of us can examine the merit of it, since it has had such
a devastating effect.  Even more importantly, I urge you to make your
copy of Barbara Taylor's missive available to any and all other postmasters
that you may believe her to have written to asking to revoke other
persons' Internet access.  You may be surprised at the systematicity
revealed by your harvest.

Since my own access was revoked in mid April at Northwestern
University's ACNS (postmaster: postmaster@casbah.acns.nwu.edu; system
admin: Roger Safian, roger@casbah.acns.nwu.edu; Director: Anne
Stunden, anns@nwu.edu), I have reason to presume that Barbara Taylor
targeted me as well.  Unfortunately, ACNS has chosen to stonewall and
not disclose the source or the content of the complaint(s) against me,
only noting in one (1) phone conversation (Roger) at the time of the
notification of account disabling that the action was for the tone of my
postings on rap.  I respectfully submit that the tone of my postings
on rap is not a matter of NU or ACNS policy, but that a message from
Barbara Taylor to that effect, if there was one, might have been the one
complaint for ACNS to field -- and therefore claim as reason enough, on
numbers, not merit, to pull my access.  If so, it becomes a burning
issue if Taylor wrote to ACNS, and if so, how she wrote, and if she
wrote as she wrote to you, at Clemson.  This is why ACNS folks are cc'd
on this letter, and I urge you to communicate your experiences with
one another.

In other words, the postmasters have it in their best interest to
realize when there is a "mass mailing", so to speak, by an
unprincipaled person, aimed to disable others' access.

I have also been told by another affected user that his system
administration received a request from Taylor to have his account
pulled.  His institution is located in Utah.

There may have been another case, at Drew U., as a user signaled being
forced to leave, but I have not investigated it yet.

Academic computing and system administration professionals should
realize that acting automatically on malicious complaints may expose
them to civil action for damages, based on alledged negligence.

In the interest of openness, public discussion, please publish the letter
from Barbara Taylor to postmaster@math.clemson.edu, and please do so without
delay.  I am copying several people on this letter to start the process of
examining this issue lest it become more confrontational still.  Far-sighted
policy needs to be adopted to prevent abuses of the "I will have your
account yanked becasue I don't like you."  Looking back on my own complaints,
I regret even giving the excuse to the institution to pull an account, and
would rather, with benefit of hindsight, suffer the long-term harassment than
be even obliquely involved in institutional censorship of free speech.

Sincerely,

				-- Marek Lugowski
				   5445 Sheridan Road #3003
				   Chicago, IL 60640

