In article <2q1o62$8js@Venus.mcs.com> karl@MCS.COM (Karl Denninger) writes:
>In article <2q1n16$lme@senator-bedfellow.mit.edu>,
>Seth Finkelstein <sethf@athena.mit.edu> wrote:
>
>Uh, excuse me, but if I come home some evening and find some jerk selling
>crack cocaine on my front steps you can bet that I'm going to call the cops!

	But will you call the cops if you have a friend over, and they
hand-roll a cigarette that smells funny?

>I don't see why this is "troubling".  If MIT staff saw something they
>believed to be lawbreaking, and had reasonable cause to do so, I see 
><NO PROBLEM WHATSOEVER> with their getting the authorities involved.

	Well, it has to do with MIT not being Singapore. In general,
there is the the concept of degree of severity. Everything is not a
Federal case. For MIT to *solicit prosecution* is extremely rare.

>The cops check things out and find no probable cause all tht time.  But
>this time, they found not just what they believed constituted probable
>cause, but the material for a federal indictment!

	The cops check things out and find probable cause which is
later shown to be based on misunderstanding or ignorance, all the
time. But this is after someone's life has been seriously disrupted.

>Can you explain to me just what is "unwise" about this procedure?

	Does the Steve Jackson Games case mean anything to you? Do you
think everyone understands account-vs-person issues? Do you have any
trouble at all with the possibility of causing someone to lose their
job or fail all their classes over a mistake? Or is that acceptable
"collateral damage" in the cause of insuring no-one ever, ever gets a
bootleg copy of SimCity 2000?
	Applying the blunt instrument of the law to an unclear, highly
technical incident should be done only in extraordinary cases. The
court system is overburdened enough as it is, nobody involved needs
more work. The costs involved in mistakes must be taken into account.
The legal system should be invoked as a last resort, not a first one.

>The machine in question was NOT the defendant's property, and he was not
>using it for the purpose to which he had license for.  That alone is an
>egregious violation of the understanding that MIT and the defendant had.

	Violating MIT's computer use policy, by itself, does NOT
warrant a charge carrying the potential of FIVE YEARS IN JAIL. In
fact, normally all that happens is that the person is told not to do
so again. Your view may be warped by the common outlook that
suspension or expulsion is an appropriate response for any "policy
violation", but that is not the case at MIT. MIT is an academic
system, and encourages experimentation. A lot of it goes on, some
clearly laudable, some in a gray area, and some crosses into the dark
side. This is the first MIT-student computer incident that has become
a criminal case, but far from the first "not using it for the purpose
to which he had license for".

>That alone might not be criminal, but it certainly is going to raise the
>ire of the administration, as well it should.

	Actually, compared to some of the other things that have
happened, the "ire" factor is nonexistent. The machines could have
been fixed with a few commands, without a crisis or even serious
inconvenience to anyone. One maintenance person would have been
annoyed, and two workstations would have been down for around a hour.
Compare that to a case where a guy sent massive chain letters all
over, crashing the mail servers and causing several system
administrators to spend many hours (days?) cleaning up the mess (I now
point people to this incident as proof-positive that chain letters do
not bring good luck :-)). It was the clearest case of "computer
recklessness" I've ever seen in my life. The offender got yelled at by
many people, and had to make some restitution, but was not prosecuted
for mail fraud by the Feds. He wasn't even suspended, and in fact
later used the notoriety to win a local contest (after all, everyone
knew his name, since they had gotten a letter from him ...).
	I go into this in such detail to illustrate the point that MIT
is not in the habit of looking to make criminal cases against
rule-breakers, even when on a personal level people might want to do so.

>I find nothing wrong <in any sense of the word> with asking for legal
>assistance when there is evidence of lawless behavior which harms another
>(in this case the software publishers involved) staring me in the face.

	Do you ask for legal assistance to prosecute people using PGP
on your system, since it may be harming the patent holders? This isn't
so hypothetical, since PGP is mentioned in the LaMacchia indictment.
Note the issue here isn't "assistance", it is "prosecution". I do find
something wrong with inappropriate use of the criminal justice system.

