Robert A. Hayden <hayden@krypton.mankato.msus.edu> wrote:
>when the person asked me for the name of the machine, I said something 
>like "You might laugh, but are there any restrictions?"
>
>"As long as it's not dirty."
>
>Well, my name wasn't dirty, and I wasn't going to raise a rukus there, 
>but I got to thinking later, with all the concern about censorship and 
>'offensive material', does machine names fall into that realm?  
>
>So at the same time we worry about protecting what's IN machines, do we 
>also need to worry about what we CALL machines?

Hmmm...that's interesting.  I've never given machines names that much
thought in this particular context, but I guess it would be trivial
to use that platform for controversial speech.

Should that particular form be restricted?  Offhand, I would think so.

One of the most commonly heard arguments about offensive speech is 
that the listener/end-user can simply "turn it off" or "ignore it."  
In fact, this (in my opinion) is the most potent argument against
censorship of individual viewpoints.  That isn't the case with a 
machine name.  If I want to connect to that machine, I must type its 
name; the same applies for email, NFS mounts or almost any other 
interaction with that machine.  

Personally, I have no desire to work with "asshole.engr.uky.edu" in
any way.  I can just hear myself telling users, "Oh, that software is 
over on asshole," or asking "You route your mail through asshole, right?"

No way.  Within the online community, that would seem to force others 
to use the controversial name you choose.  I don't believe that it 
should be considered protected speech.

I apply the same rationale to userids.  I will not create a userid
that does not have some connection with the user's name.  I don't
care if you want "morgan," "jwmorgan," "jwmorg," "wes," "wesmorgan,"
or "jwm" - but I'm not about to let you choose "killfags" as your
userid.  That would force other people to adopt your controversial
words on a regular basis.  I just can't see it.

--Wes

