In article <2q67gk$4mh@fnnews.fnal.gov>, sergey@fndaug.fnal.gov (Gary Sergey) writes:
> So is this an AUP violation, and on who's part ?  If I'm the one
> at the government site who acquired the data from the com network via
> the NSF lines, then am I the one who violated the AUP ?

I asked a similar question of NSF several years ago (I still have the
letter in a file somewhere here).  Steve Wolff has publicly said there
is no such thing as an attractive nuisance as far as the NSF AUP is
concerned.

A bookstore that accepts orders via e-mail across the NSFnet, or a company
that has commercial information in their WWW server is not a violation of
the AUP.  It is the responsibility of the user sending the e-mail to
the bookstore, or retrieving information from the WWW server to restrict
his or her usage to acceptable purposes. 

Or put it another way.  If the government says its employees should not
use U.S. government telephones to call 1-900 numbers, it is the responsibility
of the government employees not to call 1-900 numbers.  Not of the 1-900
provider to screen calls from government offices.

P.S. there is an escape hatch in the NSF AUP for other U.S. government
agencies.  Anything acceptable to the Department of Energy (Fermi Lab's
boss/owner) is acceptable on NSFNET.  If the DOE/FermiLab doesn't fire you
for getting something from a commercial WWW server, NSF isn't going to do
anything to you either.  Not that this matters for much longer, hopefully
the NSF AUP should become completely moot in another six months.
