Two email correspondents have told me that much of the Norwegian
universities' fear is caused by a book by Jon Bing and Dag Elgesem,
"Universiteters ansvar for bruk av datanett" (The liability of
universities for their use of computer networks) published by Tano in
1992 (ISBN 82-518-3087-7).

The correspondents say that
   the books says that 
     the Norwegian Penal Code says that 
        the person who owns a computer node is as liable as the editor
        of a newspaper for all material on that node.

But, ...

_____________
1. Is the material in question really illegal under the Penal code?

Of the material I've seen mentioned

  Doctor Fun cartoons <http://sunsite.unc.edu/Dave/drfun.html>
  MPEG Movie Archive  <http://www.eeb.ele.tue.nl/mpeg/index.html>
  Gif and things      <http://olt.et.tudelft.nl/fun/pictures/pictures.html>

surely it is not all illegal?
  
_____________
2. Even if some of the material is illegal, does the book and
the Penal code really make it illegal to merely point to that material?

If so, then you had better erase this message from your computer
since it points to that material.

______________
3. Is the book correct?

I realize that Jon Bing is an expert in computer law, but is he an
expert in free experession/censorship law?

In the U.S., a university is *not* legally responsible for a student
newspaper that it takes no editorial control of. Likewise a library
and bookstore is generally not legally responsible for the books in
its collection.  Likewise, a computer service is not legally
responsible for what is said on its system if it exercises no
editorial control.

I'm enclosing references.

_______________
* Does the law need to be changed?

If the book is correct about the law, then in my opinion the law needs
to be changed. Part of academic freedom is that faculty and students
can speak for themselves. If the book is correct, this law outlaws
part of academic freedom. As more and more of academic effort is
on-line, more and more academic freedom will be outlawed.


