In article <2q67gk$4mh@fnnews.fnal.gov> sergey@fndaug.fnal.gov (Gary Sergey) writes:
>This is a rather petty question, and may be more appropriate for 
>another newsgroup, but I thought I'd start here.
>
>I've noticed there's quite a few commercial web servers.  Although
>they may originate from commercial networks (e.g., CIX), their data
>certainly traverses backbones with Acceptable Use Policies (say NSF).
>
>So is this an AUP violation, and on who's part ?  If I'm the one
>at the government site who acquired the data from the com network via
>the NSF lines, then am I the one who violated the AUP ?
>
>Don't get me wrong, I welcome commercial web servers - I'm just a
>bit curious is all (and maybe a bit anal retentive).

The most recent NSFnet backbone AUP that I've seen contains a clause 
along the lines of "Traffic acceptable to associated networks
is acceptable to us whether or not it meets our normal criteria".

So, traffic generated by organizations with "commercial" access 
to Internet (providers with commercial access clauses in their
AUP's) may be passed over the NSFnet backbone.

