Part of the arguments in this discussion have been somewhat
baffling.  As an archive site moderator it seems clear to me that it
is the site who pays for its resources, that is the university, who
is fully entitled not to carry material which it does not wish to
carry.  

This remotely reminds me of one incident I had long ago at our
archive site, Garbo.  I declined one of the submitted uploads to our
archives (the reason is irrelevant).  The (American) author claimed
that I was violating his (constitutional?) rights to get his
material distributed.  That is utterly ridiculous.  There are no
such rights to get one's material distributed at the cost of
autonomous entities.  What the users and uploaders have only are
privileges.  If we decide that something will not be carried, that's
it!

To take a parallel.  Free speech does not mean that a newspaper has
to publish whatever is sent to it.  Newspapers have editors who
decide what is carried and what not. 

