From caf-talk Caf May  7 13:14:36 1994
From: brad@clarinet.com (Brad Templeton)
Subject:  The 10 Commandments of USENET
Date: Sat, 7 May 1994 06:39:33 GMT
Message-ID: <CpF6Hx.LJC@clarinet.com>

USENET, as an anarchy, has no government.  But it does have order, rules
and systems.   They're sparse, as they should be in an anarchy, but I
will list them here as I see them.   I am going to list only those that
are not controversial.  For example, the newsgroup creation system isn't
here.

1) Thou shalt not be deliberately or maliciously disruptive of the net

2) Thou shalt not cancel the postings of others, unless they violate these
commandments.

3) Thou shalt not forge the identity of others on your messages

4) Thou shalt honour the standard for the format of USENET messages and keep
   it holy -- though not so holy that you forget it's meant to be
   extensible.

5) Thou shalt not post material that has no relevance whatsoever to a
   newsgroup simply to gain a wider audience.

6) Thou shalt not mailbomb or sendsys bomb or otherwise use the network
   (E-mail or news) as an electronic weapon.

7) Thou shalt not forge or apply an approval tag to a message in a
   moderated group without the authority.

8) Thou shalt crosspost rather than post a message multiple times unless
   thou has a very good reason.


The following are also true because of the laws of the outside world.

9) Thou shalt not post the copyrighted works of others without their
   permission.

10) Thou shalt not do libel, or if thou dost, thou shalt be liable for it.


There are other commandments, for admins, are mostly derived from
the 4th commandment.

a) Thou shalt not set up a news to mail gateway that can bounceth messages
back at any USENET poster or the newsgroup because of mailer problems.

b) Thou shalt not write software to post followups without a valid
References line

c) Thou shalt be very careful that any software that might pass its ill
effects out to the net is tested in a smaller subnet before trying it live.

d) Thou shalt not use the "all" distribution in feeds outside your site.
-- 
