This is so broad that I think it violates the University's commitment
to free expression. It make you responsible for the possible actions
of another. Giving a program to another person is expression and
should be protected as far as possible. Almost any program *could*
result in damage depending on what that other person does with it.

I suggest something along the lines of the _Brandenburg v.  Ohio_
Supreme Court decision which said that expression may not be
suppressed or punished unless it is intended to produce 'imminent
lawless action' and it is 'likely to produce such action.'

