I don't know all the details of this case, nor am I a lawyer, but
let's apply common logic to this.  Like in Norway, the student
papers in Finland are owned by the students.  Let's see what that
would imply here.  Their being students does not shift the
responsibility nor the decision making to the university, because
the papers are independent of the university, and most importantly
they do not use the resources of the university in publishing the
paper.  Thus in that parallel the reponsibility lies with the
students, not the university.

:If students can have editorial control of whole newspapers, they

But note carefully that they do not have the editorial control as
students of the university, but they are students who have their own
newspaper, and thus have the editorial control.  The distinction
seems small, but it is important. 

:should also be allowed editorial control of their own World-Wide Web
:pages.

If their system is totally independent of the university's
resources, and they pay all of the activity by themselves, then that
seems a rational statement.  Their being students is not enough of a
condition for an university taking action.  But if the university
provides any part of the resources to the students's connection
(like their userids!), then the university has a say.  This is the
locig I would use if this problem occurred here. 

