In article <CpEECH.KI8@cs.uiuc.edu>, kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie) writes:
|> [stuff deleted]
|> Frankly, I doubt that money is the issue. The issues are likely
|> 
|> 1) Does the Penal Code make computer owners legally responsible for
|>        material they have no editorial control of?

I finally made it to my office and looked up the Bing&Elgesem book. The
opinion of the book is _no_ wrt the penal code, but "may be" wrt civil
law. In other words the owner may be liable to pay compensation. (Ref.
Bing&Elgesem p 71 and p 74)  Note that this is based on a number of
preconditions and assumptions that may or may not hold in a specific
case. Not being a lawyer I'll restrain myself from trying to elaborate
on this point.

However, you phrase your question in a clever way. What does "no editorial
control" imply?  An analogy (that wouldn't work in a legal argument, but
never mind) is a car owner leaving his vehicle with the keys in the lock.
Is it ok to leave a networked computer system unattended?

This winter a Norwegian BBS owner was arrested and his computer system
containing child porn and other illegal material was seized. If the case
ends up in court we'll probably have more than legal opinions to relate
to wrt your question. (However, this guy was not a university, and he
knew what material that was on his system.)

|> 2) Should the principles of academic freedom applied to university
|>        libraries, student newspapers, and other traditional forums be
|>        applied to computer forums?

I suspect the phrase "academic freedom" may have more than one interpretation,
and it's probably not identical to eg "freedom of speech."  But, the question
is important.

If the editors of a student newspaper decided to print a large number of
pictures breaking copyright law they would be in trouble. If the student's
film club distributed illegal material to the public they would be in trouble
as well. Te editors may usually refuse to print any submitted material without
having to name a reason.

Another problem is the working environment. By viewing offending material
in a lab where others are supposed to work as well you may end up with being
accused of for example sexual harassment.

Finally, computer forums may be very different in nature compared to the
"traditional" forums. A key problem is what should be considered _printed_
and how to handle multimedia stuff including moving pictures and sound.

|> I hope the answer to the first question is "no" and the answer
|> to the second is "yes".

I'd prefer to make the questions a little more specific before giving a
yes/no answer or simplified opinions.

