In article <csmith.768099186@citadel.cit.gu.edu.au>, Colin Smith wrote:
> Because of certain "limitations of resources", guess what our wonderful
> sysops are doing to our NN?
> 
> They're cutting off our access to ALL alt.* newsgroups !!!  Does this
> suck, or what?  Even worse, no major members of faculty staff were
> consulted in this decision (and, of course, no students were consulted
> -- that would have made too much sense).
> 
> Your comments please.
> 
Two interesting things emerge from studying "Total traffic through uunet
for the last 2 weeks" recently posted to news.lists.  First usenet
traffic has doubled in less than one year.  Second half of the traffic
is in alt.* newsgroups.  There are several ways to cope with increasing
volumes of news in the face of limited resources - the two major
limitations are probably disk storage and bandwidth.

If disk storage is a problem then expiry times can be reduced, but only
up to a point.  If expiry times are too short then most articles expire
before they arrive. Once expiry times have been reduced to a minimum, or
if bandwidth is the problem then the only other alternative is to cut
down on the volume of news being dealt with.  If current growth rates
continue then cutting out all alt.* newsgroups will give a breathing
space of something less than one year.

The other alternative is to increase the resources devoted to supporting
the news system - but who pays?

There are some serious issues here - I have no idea what the best
solutions are.  Anyone else have comments?

Michael.
