Well, the U.S. seems to be a world leader in such cases. Here a
university is pretty safe from liablity if it provides an effective
harassment-complaint procedure that ends (and sometimes punishes)
harassment.  Note, that merely "to be offended" is not necessarily "to
be harassed". To be harassed, the offensive must unresasonably
interfere with an individual's work.

Thus, planting a tree outside someone's office is not harassment even
if they say the tree offends them, because that offense is not
reasonable.

Thus, having a copy of _Mein Kampf_ in the University library (it is
in many university libraries in Norway and the U.S.) is not harassment
because those it offends are need not read it.

For more on the difference in the U.S. between harassing speech and
protected speech see the enclosed on-line reference. Or, if you are at
the University of Bergan, I think you can read the lastest news in the
