From caf-talk Caf May  8 14:50:15 1994
Newsgroups: comp.admin.policy,alt.comp.acad-freedom.talk,no.general
From: blix@asimov.cs.uiuc.edu (Gunnar Blix)
Subject: Re: A university in Norway bans references to offensive material????
Message-ID: <CpHyo3.157@cs.uiuc.edu>
Date: Sun, 8 May 1994 18:43:14 GMT

oddms@eik.ii.uib.no (OddMagne Sekkingstad) writes:

>|> You are a professor. The U. of Vaasa provides you with extensive
>|> resources including an office and your salary. By your logic,
>|> everything you say, write, or publish (at least on campus) needs to be
>|> approved by the university.

>I guess that the university would throw him out, if he use his
>office and his salary to distribute childpornography, or any
>other illegal activities.

That's not the point here.  This is clearly regulated by the
institution of tenure in U.S. universities (I think) and by
Professorships (which are by definition tenured) in Norway at least -
a tenured Professor can only be dismissed for neglecting his duties or
breaking the law, through due legal process.

On the other hand, tenure protects the Professor for being pursued for
things the University finds inappropriate.  This is Carl's question,
in essence.  If a Professor decides to publish a new scientific theory
that breaks with what the University believes and condones, he is
within his right to do so and cannot be fired for it, unless it also
breaks the law.  This was also essentially the argument about the
anti-abortion Bishops in Norway (not very) recently.

The interesting question is whether the University should be able to
ban material that is not illegal, but may be offensive, and of which
it disapproves.  For a Professor, the answer is clearly no.  For
students, it may be different.  And the next question is then whether
this should include pointers to where such material can be found, even
if the pointers are indirect.

>|> Even if your university could legally require this, would it be wise
>|> to?

>Yes.

If it does, in some sense it exercises editorial control and would be
more likely to be held liable for material retained.  If it doesn't,
the argument would be that the lack of editorial control shifts the
responsibility to the users.  Probably there is no legal precedence
for this as of now in the Nordic countries; in the U.S. this was
essentially the verdict in one such case.

So perhaps it is not so wise after all.

