Carl has a strong interest in the topic of freedom of information.
Unlike what P. C. Jorgensen seems to imply here, it is not Carl's
intention to become the new abirdson of soc.culture.nordic.  He is
simply curious as to what another country does in situations like
these.

So far, noone really seems to answer (or perhaps, understand) what his
question is all about, namely how Norwegian Law feels about the
Universities' editorial responsibilities for material that is merely
offensive (to some) and not illegal (according to Norwegian law) on
University computers.  Secondarily, he would also like to know if it
is the case that Jon Bing and other legal experts believe indirect
pointers (such as a general pointer to the MPEG movie archive) to
potentially illegal material are themselves illegal, and if so, if the
University can be held responsible for them, and therefore should
exercise editorial control.

If anyone has blown this discussion out of proportion, I feel it
those who have merely pointed out what they feel the answers should be
in their opinion, without checking the facts or answering his
questions.

Carl is, in reality, very moderate, and would have no problems dealing
with P. C.'s opinion if it was presented to him in a language he could
read.  If you feel he's gotten out of hand, why don't you tell him?
